/* Detailed teaching material supplements, rather than replaces, the original lessons. */
const lessonDetails = [];
function detail(id,explanation,worked,f401,h7,expected,troubleshoot,questions) {
 lessonDetails.push({id,explanation,worked,boards:{f401,h7},expected,troubleshoot,questions});
}
detail(1,
 ['Define the failure as an observable contract: a heartbeat must advance, a packet must be acknowledged, or a deadline must be met. State the observation window and measurement method. A CPU stopped in HAL_Delay is a location, not a diagnosis; the next question is whether the tick can advance during the experiment.',
 'Keep a hypothesis log with prediction, intervention and outcome. Prefer an experiment that can reject an explanation over one that merely makes the symptom disappear. Preserve the failing ELF before editing; a changed binary changes the object being investigated.'],
 'Suppose the main heartbeat stops but a timer ISR counter continues. This rejects a completely stopped processor during that window. If a parser checkpoint remains at entry, inspect the parser exit conditions. If both counters stop, inspect masks, exception state and reset history before blaming the parser.',
 ['Generate a NUCLEO-F401RE project with SysTick and a TIM2 update interrupt at 10 Hz. Increment separate volatile main and ISR counters.','Run freely for ten seconds, halt once, and save counters, PC, PRIMASK and the current exception number.','Temporarily add a controlled infinite loop in main after the fifth checkpoint. Confirm whether TIM2 still advances; restore the baseline afterward.'],
 ['Use the M7 core on a NUCLEO-H743ZI2 and record its actual clock from the generated configuration. Generate the same 10 Hz TIM2 event independently of the F401 prescaler.','Repeat the main-loop stall without adding UART logging; compare counters and the active exception.','If using a dual-core board, record whether the other core is running. Restrict this first experiment to one core so its observations have one interpretation.'],
 'During the intentional main-loop stall, the main counter stays fixed while the enabled timer counter advances. A halted debugger stops CPU execution, so compare free-running windows rather than repeatedly sampling a stopped system.',
 'If neither counter advances, verify interrupts are enabled, the vector is correct and the timer source is running. If reset occurred, record reset flags before startup code clears them; restarted counters cannot describe the pre-reset sequence.',
 [['How would you choose between logging and a GPIO probe?','Use logging for structured low-rate state when buffering and bandwidth are adequate. Use a GPIO edge to correlate timing with external signals. Measure the observation overhead and ensure a full log buffer cannot block the failing path.'],['What evidence would reject a deadlock hypothesis?','Show the supposedly blocked participants making progress under the original trigger, or demonstrate a different causal mechanism. A single successful run under changed timing does not reject an intermittent deadlock.']]);
detail(2,
 ['Separate stimulus generation from the code under test. A fixed payload, seed, rate and reset procedure turn a story into a repeatable experiment. Record counters even when the test passes so that silent truncation does not look like success.',
 'For an intermittent result, report failures/attempts and the observation duration. Changing the environment to accelerate reproduction is useful only if the resulting evidence supports the same mechanism; an overload invented by the test may be a different defect.'],
 'For a 64-byte destination with no terminator requirement, expected accepted/rejected byte counts are 63/0, 64/0 and 64/1 for lengths 63, 64 and 65. If the API also needs a terminator, usable payload capacity becomes 63; state that different contract explicitly.',
 ['Call append_byte with deterministic values from 0 to n-1 for n=0,1,63,64,65, resetting used and rejected for each case.','Record the final accepted count, rejection count and checksum of accepted bytes.','Run the matrix after a cold boot and after a debugger reset; save which initialization steps execute in each mode.'],
 ['Run the same pure buffer matrix on H743 with identical source and test vectors. Keep peripheral DMA out of the initial comparison.','Then repeat with a real UART producer and record actual received lengths and errors so the transport cannot silently alter the stimulus.','Preserve separate F401/H7 build IDs, clocks and board revisions in the results.'],
 'The pure buffer tests yield identical logical outcomes on both boards. Differences appearing only with UART traffic identify a separate integration investigation.',
 'If the 65th byte appears accepted, verify the test resets used and that your debugger is displaying the current symbol. If traffic is missing before append_byte, instrument the receive path instead of changing the capacity check.',
 [['Why preserve the stimulus seed?','It lets another run generate the same sequence and investigate the first divergent operation. A seed is insufficient unless the generator algorithm and version are also preserved.'],['Does a cold boot equal a debugger reset?','Not necessarily. Power, retained RAM, peripheral reset domains, boot options and debugger initialization can differ. Document the actual reset procedure and inspect relevant state.']]);
detail(3,
 ['Draw the data path and define one measurable contract at every boundary. A successful peripheral transaction does not establish that its payload reached the application, and a visible UI update does not establish the source value was fresh.',
 'Use substitutions with a recorded purpose. A synthetic sensor sample tests the downstream path but bypasses bus timing, interrupt handling and DMA. Restore real input when moving upstream so that a passing substitute is not mistaken for complete system validation.'],
 'Record counters for RX interrupt, bytes enqueued, frames parsed and messages published. If counts are 200, 200, 10 and 8, investigate the parse-to-publish boundary first. Also inspect rejection counters: two intentionally invalid frames could explain the apparent discrepancy.',
 ['Create a four-stage path: synthetic sequence, bounded buffer, parser and final counter. Give each stage an input/output count.','Suppress one publish operation every tenth frame in a test-only wrapper. Write the prediction before running 100 frames.','Move the synthetic source to the parser input, then to the buffer input. Explain exactly which stages each run covers.'],
 ['Repeat the synthetic pipeline on H743 without DMA and establish its count invariant.','Introduce UART DMA only after the baseline passes; add DMA completion and consumed-buffer counts.','Verify memory placement and cache handoff separately if the first divergence moves to the DMA-to-CPU boundary.'],
 'The injected publisher fault yields 100 valid parsed frames and 90 published outputs. Every difference is explained by a counter, rather than inferred from a missing terminal message.',
 'If all counters agree but displayed data is stale, count fresh sequence IDs at the display boundary. If instrumentation itself drops records, reduce record size or rate and track log overflow.',
 [['Why not replace every subsystem with a stub at once?','That may show the application can run, but it cannot localize which removed component caused the original failure. Change one boundary and track which hypotheses each experiment tests.'],['When is binary isolation inappropriate?','When the failure depends on interactions removed by the split, such as timing or shared ownership. Preserve the interaction while adding boundary observations, or model it explicitly in the substitute.']]);
detail(4,
 ['Distinguish the introducing defect, the triggering input and the final symptom. An oversized packet is an input; the defect may be failure to reserve a terminator byte; a corrupted adjacent state field explains the later timeout. A causal chain connects all three.',
 'Try a counterfactual check: with the same input and environment, does correcting only the demonstrated defect remove the violation? Then test legal behavior nearby. A patch that rejects all inputs removes the symptom while breaking the product contract.'],
 'A char buffer[8] can hold at most seven payload characters plus a zero terminator. Length 7 writes the terminator at index 7; length 8 must be rejected. The defect report should include these exact indices and the checked capacity contract.',
 ['Use an eight-byte char array with the checked terminate function. Test lengths 0,7,8 using known contents.','Save a memory view before/after each valid call; verify the rejected call does not modify any byte.','Document the boundary bug as a mechanism without executing an intentional out-of-range C access.'],
 ['Repeat the eight-byte terminator test on H743 and compare results under -Og and -O2.','Inspect the optimized branch and store in disassembly, then explain how the capacity contract remains the same.','Attach the exact H7 ELF rather than resolving its addresses with the F401 artifact.'],
 'Lengths 0 and 7 succeed, 8 fails, and the rejection preserves the original array. The validated fix protects the contract under both build configurations.',
 'If behavior differs under optimization, first check the harness for uninitialized storage, format mismatch and other undefined behavior. A different instruction sequence alone does not invalidate the fix.',
 [['What makes evidence sufficient to close a defect?','A reproducible pre-fix failure, a demonstrated causal mechanism, a focused correction and relevant regression evidence. State untested conditions and residual uncertainty, especially for intermittent failures.'],['Can there be more than one root cause?','Yes. Separate causal chains can produce the same symptom. Track independent reproducers and avoid claiming one correction covers all reports unless their evidence matches.']]);
detail(5,
 ['Separate execution control from trace. Halt/step controls stop the core; SWV/ITM can expose selected runtime events when the core, probe, clock configuration and SWO wiring support them. Neither feature replaces a logic analyzer for observing the electrical interface.',
 'When connection fails, work outward from power and reset to transport and then application behavior. A low SWD clock can help diagnose signal integrity, but does not repair a wrong pin, absent ground or target held in reset. Preserve recoverable access before repurposing debug pins.'],
 'If the debugger reads the core identity but cannot halt after firmware starts, compare connect-under-reset with normal attach. Success under reset suggests the running image changes access conditions, enters low power or resets rapidly; it does not prove flash is damaged.',
 ['Use the onboard ST-LINK on F401RE; identify its debug connection and USB connector from the board manual.','Record core identity and halt at main. Inspect PC, MSP, xPSR and VTOR.','Try attach to an already-running known-good image, then compare the reset-and-halt path without changing option bytes.'],
 ['Use H743ZI2 onboard ST-LINK and select the actual H743 device in the launch configuration.','Inspect core identity and the loaded symbols; verify this is an M7 session rather than assuming an M4 configuration can be reused.','For a dual-core H7, document which debug session controls each core and which reset action affects the other core.'],
 'The core identity, selected device, vector table and ELF symbols agree. Attach and reset-and-halt produce documented, potentially different startup observations.',
 'If SWV is empty while breakpoints work, check SWO routing, trace clock and ITM stimulus configuration rather than rewiring SWD. If the target is not detected, return to target voltage reference, reset and SWD signals.',
 [['Does SWO need the application UART?','No. SWO is a trace output with separate routing and configuration. UART logs can coexist, but their baud settings and pins do not configure ITM/SWV.'],['Why inspect VTOR during bring-up?','It identifies the vector-table location used for exceptions. A bootloader or relocated image can make the default flash-base assumption wrong; verify vectors against the actual loaded application.']]);
detail(6,
 ['Source breakpoints describe requested locations; the debugger resolves them to executable instructions. Empty or inlined functions can share locations. When a line contains several operations, instruction stepping is the only precise way to say which operation executed.',
 'A breakpoint changes concurrency. Other bus masters, external peers and some peripherals may continue while the CPU is stopped. Use the device debug-freeze controls deliberately for timers/watchdogs and record which components remain active.'],
 'A UART peer transmits 100 bytes while the CPU is halted. The peripheral and DMA may receive data, but the parser cannot drain it. A buffer overflow observed after resume can be caused by the breakpoint experiment, even if normal free-running execution was correct.',
 ['Break inside the ten-iteration loop and compare source stepping with instruction stepping.','Run the same code without breakpoints and inspect only the final iteration count.','For a configured timer, compare counter behavior while halted with its documented debug-freeze option enabled and disabled.'],
 ['Repeat on H743 and record whether DMA and the selected timer continue while the M7 core is halted.','Use a short finite UART transfer to avoid unbounded incoming traffic during the comparison.','If another core is active, observe its independent heartbeat and document whether the debug configuration halts both cores.'],
 'Instruction stepping advances the expected PC positions. Peripheral/other-core activity during halt is measured rather than assumed.',
 'If step-over never returns, check whether the called function waits on an event that the halted/debug environment prevents. Avoid repeatedly stopping a system whose bug depends on normal scheduling.',
 [['Is a conditional breakpoint always evaluated on target?','No. Some implementations halt on each hit and evaluate the condition on the host, which can severely disturb timing. Check the debugger and probe capabilities.'],['Why can a watchdog reset occur during a breakpoint?','The watchdog may keep running while the CPU is halted unless the relevant debug-freeze behavior is configured. Record reset reason and device-specific freeze settings.']]);
detail(7,
 ['Begin with an object whose address and lifetime are stable. Watch a global at its linked address before trying an optimized local that may live in a register. A write watchpoint can stop even if a store writes the same value, depending on debugger watch semantics.',
 'A watchpoint answers a narrow question about matching accesses observed by its debug hardware. It is not a memory firewall. Bus-master writes and other-core accesses need their own observation and ownership strategy.'],
 'If a field at 0x20000104 changes, first resolve that address from the current ELF. A four-byte comparator at 0x20000100 does not necessarily cover it. Verify access width and address range, then inspect the instruction before/at the reported stop.',
 ['Set the write watchpoint after C startup has initialized configuration. Call change_configuration once and inspect the stack.','Repeat watching a neighboring aligned global; verify that an unrelated write does not satisfy your intended watched range.','Delete the watchpoint and measure the free-running test separately.'],
 ['Watch a CPU-owned global on H743 first, then compare with a dedicated DMA-owned buffer.','Record which agent writes each region and whether its write is visible to the core watchpoint.','When a DMA buffer changes without a trap, inspect DMA destination/count and cache state instead of adding more CPU watchpoints blindly.'],
 'The CPU writer is located reproducibly. A missing DMA-triggered watchpoint is treated as a hardware-observation limitation, not proof of no write.',
 'If GDB cannot insert the watchpoint, reduce its byte range, check alignment and remove unused comparators. If the watched symbol moves or disappears, use an appropriate symbolized build and a stable object.',
 [['How would you catch a use-after-return write?','First correct the object lifetime model. For investigation, use a stable captured address only while its mapping is understood and correlate the writer with scope/lifetime evidence. Reused stack slots can create misleading watchpoint hits.'],['What does a watchpoint stop prove?','That a matching observed access occurred, subject to comparator semantics. It does not by itself prove the access violated the object contract or was the first corrupting action.']]);
detail(8,
 ['Distinguish load address, runtime address and symbol address. Initialized data is typically stored in a flash load image and copied to its RAM runtime address by startup. .bss consumes RAM but typically no payload bytes in a raw flash image. Inspect the linker script alongside the map.',
 'Disassembly supplies actual access widths, offsets and branches. Debug source lines provide context, but optimized/inlined code may need instruction-level analysis. Keep the toolchain version, build flags and exact ELF with a crash report.'],
 'If .data has 128 bytes and .bss has 1,024 bytes, these sections require 1,152 bytes of static RAM. A size report does not include every possible stack peak or heap allocation. If a load uses base 0x20000020 with offset 12, inspect address 0x2000002C and its region.',
 ['Locate .text, .data and .bss in the F401 map and compare the runtime/load addresses with the linker MEMORY regions.','Resolve the address of a real function from your ELF using addr2line and inspect nearby instructions.','Increase one static array by 32 bytes, rebuild, and explain the map/size delta.'],
 ['Locate H743 AXI SRAM, DTCM and the selected DMA buffer in the map and linker script.','Verify that the DMA controller used by your example can reach its destination region using the device bus matrix documentation.','Compare symbol placement before/after a deliberate linker section change; retain both ELF files.'],
 'Every investigated address is resolved using its own build. Static RAM growth is explained, and a DMA placement decision is supported by the actual memory map.',
 'If addr2line returns unknown, check symbols, debug information, ELF identity and load offset. A bootloader relocation or wrong artifact is more likely than a need to guess the nearest source line.',
 [['Why is a map file not sufficient to decode source lines?','It establishes section and symbol placement but normally lacks the complete source/debug mapping carried by the ELF. Preserve both artifacts for placement and instruction/source analysis.'],['How would you identify an image in a field crash record?','Embed a stable build identifier tied to archived artifacts. A timestamp alone may collide or fail to capture source and configuration; record enough identity to select the exact ELF.']]);
detail(9,
 ['Construct a configuration-difference table before changing optimization: flags, defines, assertions, linker script, logging, LTO and libraries. A release-only failure may come from a disabled check or changed timing rather than an optimizer defect.',
 'The one-shot volatile flag example has a limited contract: one producer eventually writes one value that remains set. It cannot count events or safely implement a multi-step ownership protocol. Keep visibility, atomic access and ordering separate in your reasoning.'],
 'A 16-byte local array may be optimized away when it has no observable effect. Seeing less stack use in release is expected. By contrast, indexing past that array is undefined behavior in both builds; a debug build surviving is not evidence of correctness.',
 ['Build the same F401 one-shot example with -Og -g and -O2 -g while keeping every other configuration item fixed.','Inspect whether the polling load remains in the disassembly and whether the ISR actually executes.','Add a multiple-event scenario separately; demonstrate why a flag cannot represent event multiplicity.'],
 ['Repeat the two symbolized builds on H743 with identical clock/cache configuration.','Compare timing without changing peripheral setup, then run the same counter-based correctness checks.','If DMA is involved, treat cache ownership as a separate contract; volatile alone cannot make its memory coherent.'],
 'The valid one-shot handoff works under both builds. Event coalescing in a flag is documented, and timing differences are not mislabeled as data correctness.',
 'If release results differ, check initialization and object lifetimes before disabling optimization. Preserve the failing production ELF and inspect its actual instructions, not a rebuilt debug approximation.',
 [['What is a useful release diagnostic build?','The production optimization and memory layout with retained symbols and carefully bounded telemetry. Avoid changing many features at once; document any diagnostic change that can affect the symptom.'],['Can volatile fix a cache-coherency defect?','No. It constrains compiler treatment of accesses but does not force DMA/CPU cache ownership or maintenance. Use a validated memory and handoff design.']]);
detail(10,
 ['The vector table contains exception entry addresses; the active exception is reflected in IPSR/xPSR. Thread-mode faults enter a handler, whereas a fault during fault handling can escalate further. Keep the first fault evidence because a secondary fault may hide the initiating problem.',
 'Enable configurable faults one at a time in the teaching experiment. A disabled UsageFault can escalate to HardFault; an enabled UsageFault has its own handler. This controlled comparison explains escalation without intentionally dereferencing an arbitrary invalid C pointer.'],
 'For UDF, the UNDEFINSTR status is bit 16 of CFSR. With UsageFault disabled, HFSR.FORCED can accompany it. With UsageFault enabled and normal exception handling conditions, the UsageFault handler is expected instead. Capture before clearing sticky flags.',
 ['Put a breakpoint in the generated HardFault and UsageFault handlers. Execute one UDF in an isolated F401 image.','Save CFSR/HFSR and handler identity, reset, enable UsageFault and repeat once.','Remove injection and confirm a normal boot so the board is left recoverable.'],
 ['Repeat on the H743 M7 with the matching core manual open. Avoid combining cache/MPU experiments with this instruction fault.','Save the same status set and compare the exception routing.','On dual-core H7, inject only on the selected core and record the other core state separately.'],
 'The undefined-instruction status appears in both runs; handler routing changes as predicted by the configurable-fault enable. No arbitrary memory corruption is required.',
 'If a different handler runs, inspect vector relocation, SHCSR, interrupt masks and pre-existing sticky flags. If the debugger stops before handler entry, note its exception/vector-catch configuration.',
 [['What is exception escalation?','A fault that cannot be handled at its configured exception level can be promoted to HardFault. Inspect original status bits rather than treating HardFault as a complete diagnosis.'],['Why should fault injection be isolated?','The experiment intentionally stops normal control flow. Isolation keeps actuator state, unrelated data and recovery behavior predictable and makes the fault record easier to interpret.']]);
detail(11,
 ['On a valid basic frame, offsets 0..7 words contain R0,R1,R2,R3,R12,LR,PC,xPSR. EXC_RETURN bit 2 selects the interrupted stack, and bit 4 distinguishes basic versus extended floating-point state. An extended frame places 18 words before the basic core-register area; verify the architecture and lazy FP state before interpreting it.',
 'Frame decoding is conditional evidence. Check CFSR stacking/unstacking errors, selected-stack alignment, accessible RAM bounds and the complete required frame length before reading PC. xPSR bit 9 describes an alignment word beyond the core frame; it matters when reconstructing the pre-exception SP. Never trust a plausible PC from an invalid frame.'],
 'For a valid basic raw SP of 0x20001000, stacked PC is at raw SP+24 and xPSR at +28. For an extended frame, core-frame base is raw SP+72 and PC is at raw SP+96. These are layout calculations, not permission to read an unvalidated address.',
 ['Use the debugger on F401 with floating-point instructions excluded from the first fault experiment. Keep the generated handler and inspect LR, MSP and PSP.','Select the interrupted SP using EXC_RETURN. Check it lies in the linked RAM region and that fault status reports no stacking failure.','Read the eight-word basic frame and resolve its PC to the controlled UDF. Only then repeat with a known floating-point context.'],
 ['Perform the same basic-frame experiment on H743 with exact RAM bounds from its linker script.','If enabling FP, capture EXC_RETURN and FPCCR, then distinguish reserved/lazy FP context from valid core-register words.','Compare thread mode using MSP with a deliberately configured PSP context only after the first lab is understood; do not assume an RTOS is necessary for PSP.'],
 'A valid decoded PC points into the interrupted instruction stream. Basic and extended offsets are explicitly distinguished; invalid frames are reported as unavailable rather than dereferenced.',
 'If frame words look like handler locals, you likely sampled the current handler stack after its prologue. Capture/select the entry SP, inspect disassembly, and check whether startup symbols and ABI match the handler implementation.',
 [['Which registers are absent from the basic hardware frame?','R4-R11 are not in the basic automatically stacked frame. A task port or software prologue may save them separately; do not substitute the handler current registers without evidence.'],['How do stacking faults change your decoder?','They make the frame potentially incomplete or inaccessible. Save status and raw SP, mark frame data invalid, and avoid walking or dereferencing the damaged stack.']]);
detail(12,
 ['Decode CFSR in three groups: MMFSR in bits 0-7, BFSR in 8-15 and UFSR in 16-31. Record the raw word as well as interpreted names so that later review can correct a decoder without losing evidence. Read address registers only as meaningful when their validity bits are set.',
 'Several bits may be set from earlier events. Capture/reset experiment state deliberately and use write-one-to-clear semantics from the manual. Avoid a generic read-modify-write helper on status registers; it may acknowledge more events than intended.'],
 'CFSR=0x00008200 means PRECISERR (bit 9) and BFARVALID (bit 15). It supports using BFAR as a fault address and inspecting the faulting load/store. CFSR=0x00000400 is IMPRECISERR and does not make BFAR valid or guarantee the stacked PC identifies the initiating store.',
 ['Capture raw CFSR/HFSR from the controlled UDF on F401. Decode UNDEFINSTR and any escalation bit.','Use synthetic words 0x00008200 and 0x00000400 in the offline decoder exercise; do not force arbitrary invalid bus accesses.','Verify the original snapshot stays available before any deliberate status clear.'],
 ['Repeat raw status capture on H743 and consult the M7 fault chapter for auxiliary bus-fault evidence available on that device.','Separate the generic CFSR interpretation from implementation-specific bus/cache status.','For imprecise evidence, record the surrounding instruction stream and recent writes without claiming one exact faulting PC.'],
 'Synthetic decoder results match the documented bit meanings, and physical UDF evidence is kept separate from invented bus-fault examples.',
 'If BFAR appears outside your expected region but BFARVALID is clear, discard the address interpretation. If flags persist, verify reset behavior and whether your code cleared them with the documented semantics.',
 [['Why retain raw registers after decoding them?','The interpretation may be incomplete or the decoder may have a defect. Raw values plus core/device identity allow independent reanalysis.'],['Can an imprecise fault still be investigated?','Yes. Bound the suspect write sequence, add completion barriers only as a controlled diagnostic intervention where appropriate, isolate peripherals and inspect auxiliary evidence. Record that the reported PC may be later than the initiating transaction.']]);
detail(13,
 ['Compute an instruction effective address using the interrupted register values and actual instruction encoding. The source expression may hide several operations. Establish whether the faulting access was an instruction fetch, data read, write or exception-stack transaction before investigating a pointer.',
 'A bad consumer address is often the end of an earlier lifetime or bounds defect. Once the immediate instruction is understood, trace where its pointer/value came from. Preserve evidence that distinguishes a corrupted pointer from a legitimate pointer to unavailable hardware.'],
 'For LDR R2,[R3,#8] with stacked R3=0x20000100, the requested address is 0x20000108. Compare that with the memory map and access width. If the instruction uses R7, the basic exception frame alone is insufficient because R7 was not automatically stacked there.',
 ['Resolve the actual stacked UDF PC from the F401 lab using the matching ELF and addr2line.','Inspect the disassembly around it and identify which words are executable instructions rather than literal data.','For the synthetic LDR example, calculate the address and state which captured registers are required.'],
 ['Resolve H743 addresses using its own ELF and verify whether code executes in flash, ITCM or another mapped region.','If a bootloader relocates vectors or code, record load/runtime addresses before using offline tools.','Inspect an optimized function and compare the line mapping with the actual instruction sequence.'],
 'The known UDF resolves correctly on each board. The synthetic load calculation is 0x20000108; missing registers are explicitly recognized as missing evidence.',
 'If disassembly seems nonsensical, check Thumb code context, symbol identity and address mapping. Do not “fix” a crash report by using an ELF that produces a more plausible source line.',
 [['When would you distrust a backtrace?','After stack corruption, invalid frame selection, missing unwind/debug data or heavily optimized tail/inlined calls. Correlate it with validated stack bounds, instruction addresses and independent evidence.'],['Why inspect neighboring instructions?','A PC may identify a nearby point after a trap or an imprecise failure. Neighboring operations explain data dependencies and access sequence, but they do not justify guessing which store failed.']]);
detail(14,
 ['Validate source availability and destination capacity independently. Size arithmetic can itself overflow: prefer n <= available-header after verifying available >= header, rather than header+n <= available with unchecked addition. Keep parser validation separate from committing new application state.',
 'Object lifetime is as important as capacity. Returning a pointer to a local array, using freed storage or reusing a DMA buffer before consumption violates ownership even when every index is in range. For overlapping valid arrays, choose memmove when overlap is part of the contract.'],
 'An input reports a 60-byte payload, but only 20 bytes remain in the received frame and the destination holds 64. Capacity alone passes; source-availability validation must reject it. The application configuration should remain unchanged after rejection.',
 ['Test copy_payload with a 64-byte destination, 20 available source bytes and requested lengths 0,20,21,64,65 using valid arrays.','Fill the destination with a known pattern and verify a rejected request preserves all bytes.','Review one parser for unchecked length addition and local-pointer escape; record why each access is within a live object.'],
 ['Run the same pure tests on H743 before involving DMA.','For a DMA-backed parser, copy or transfer ownership only after completion and cache handoff, then prevent the producer from reusing the region while parsing.','Use sequence IDs to detect a buffer reused too early even when no out-of-range write occurs.'],
 'Lengths beyond source availability or destination capacity reject without mutation. A lifetime/ownership defect is distinguished from a bounds defect.',
 'If a corruption disappears after moving variables, preserve both layouts and investigate the first invalid access. Do not accept layout changes or extra padding as a capacity/lifetime correction.',
 [['Why can a checksum pass while a memory bug remains?','A checksum validates some data properties, not object bounds or lifetime. Corruption may occur after checking, in unprotected metadata, or in a buffer reused concurrently.'],['How do you avoid partial parser updates?','Validate the whole frame into temporary owned state, then commit the configuration at one defined handoff point. Keep rejection paths from mutating live state.']]);
detail(15,
 ['Build a stack budget from call paths, local storage, ABI overhead and possible exception nesting. Compiler .su files are useful input, but dynamic allocation, indirect calls, recursion and assembly can require additional analysis. Never sum every function indiscriminately; only concurrently active paths contribute.',
 'Treat MSP, each RTOS task stack and heap as separate allocations. Watermark patterns show locations observed as overwritten, not a mathematical maximum. A task high-water result depends on port/API units and whether the worst path and FP exception context actually executed.'],
 'An illustrative budget with a 256-byte active thread path plus two nested ISR software costs of 96 and 80 bytes, and two 32-byte basic frames, totals 496 bytes before margin/alignment. If thread mode uses PSP, allocate its path/frame to the appropriate PSP stack and handler nesting to MSP instead of combining them blindly.',
 ['Enable -fstack-usage on F401, inspect the deepest known main call path and two intended interrupt paths.','Use the map to identify MSP reservation and ensure static arrays do not overlap the reserved region.','Exercise error and maximum-length paths, then compare observed evidence with the static budget and assumptions.'],
 ['Repeat on H743 and record selected core, linker RAM banks and FP context assumptions.','If an RTOS is already present, log every task stack watermark and MSP evidence under interrupt stress; otherwise keep this a bare-metal budget.','Review large local buffers and replace only with explicitly owned persistent storage or a justified larger stack.'],
 'The budget identifies stack owners, nesting assumptions and margin. Measured low usage does not silently replace the planned worst-case analysis.',
 'If a watermark is implausible, verify fill timing, stack direction, bounds, units and whether the debugger modified memory. A static workspace shared by two callers introduces a reentrancy problem even if stack usage improves.',
 [['Why is recursion difficult in a real-time stack budget?','Its maximum depth must be proven and each active frame counted. If input can control an unbounded depth, no finite reservation establishes safety; use an iterative bounded design or enforce a validated depth limit.'],['How does lazy FP stacking affect measurement?','It changes when floating-point context is reserved or written, so simple fill-pattern observations and basic-frame assumptions can underdescribe the needed exception context. Include the configured FP behavior in the budget.']]);
detail(16,
 ['Read peripheral state as a dependency chain: source clock, bus/kernel clock selection, reset, pin configuration, peripheral mode, interrupt/DMA enable and status handling. A successful HAL initialization call does not prove that the external pin is connected to the intended peripheral function.',
 'Use the exact peripheral register semantics. Some fields are write-only, some are read-to-clear or cleared by a sequence, and some status flags require writing ones. A generic register-dump loop can alter the very condition you need to observe.'],
 'At 115200 baud, a bit lasts about 8.681 microseconds. An 8N1 byte uses ten serial bits, so the ideal payload rate is 11,520 bytes/s. A measured bit interval near 17.36 microseconds suggests a roughly twofold baud error, not a framing parser issue.',
 ['Choose USART2 pins routed by your F401RE schematic, or explicitly document a different UART and connector. Transmit repeated 0x55 using generated HAL setup.','Measure the TX waveform, verify 8N1 framing and compare actual bit interval with configured baud.','Check F401 RCC/GPIO/USART settings in the reference manual, noting SR/DR access side effects.'],
 ['Choose an H743 UART route from its board schematic and verify the peripheral kernel clock mux as well as the APB clock.','Generate pins and baud configuration afresh; compare 0x55 timing at the connector with the F401 result.','Use H7 ISR/RDR/TDR/ICR semantics for the selected USART rather than copying F401 status-clearing sequences.'],
 'Measured bit timing agrees with intended baud within the recorded clock/measurement tolerance, and the correct connector carries the signal.',
 'If the MCU pin toggles but the connector is silent, inspect board jumpers and routing. If timing is consistently wrong, inspect the actual peripheral clock and divisor before modifying delays or parser logic.',
 [['What distinguishes a peripheral clock bug from a pin-mux bug?','Clock/divisor faults often produce a waveform at the wrong rate; a wrong mux or route may produce no relevant waveform at the intended pin. Combine register readback with measurements at both MCU pin and connector.'],['Why preserve a register snapshot?','It records the failing configuration and status for comparison with the reference manual and a working baseline. Capture only registers whose reads are safe, and record capture timing.']]);
detail(17,
 ['Interrupt diagnosis has separate layers: event generation, source enable, pending state, NVIC enable, priority/masks, vector entry and handler acknowledgment. A pending interrupt can wait because of masking or higher-urgency activity. A continuously asserted source can immediately pend again after return.',
 'PRIMASK masks configurable exceptions; BASEPRI applies a priority threshold on supported cores. Priority numbers supplied to CMSIS are not automatically the same shifted representation used by some RTOS configuration constants. Read implemented priority bits and grouping before comparing values.'],
 'If two enabled IRQs have numerical priorities 2 and 5 under a grouping that allocates those bits to preemption, priority 2 can preempt priority 5. Equal preemption priority does not allow mutual preemption just because subpriority differs. Tail-chaining can reduce overhead but does not correct an uncleared source.',
 ['Configure F401 TIM2 update at 10 Hz, enable the NVIC entry and verify the generated handler dispatches to the matching HAL handle.','Sample source status, NVIC enable/pending and PRIMASK while diagnosing a deliberate NVIC-disable test.','Restore enable, then check callback counts over a known free-running interval without treating pending events as an unlimited queue.'],
 ['Configure H743 TIM2 from its actual timer clock and repeat the source-to-callback chain.','Add a second low-rate timer only after baseline success; use GPIO pulses to observe priority/preemption with recorded grouping.','If FreeRTOS is present, verify interrupt priorities against its permitted syscall range before any FromISR call.'],
 'A disabled NVIC blocks handler execution even if the source occurs. Restoring it permits service, but repeated elapsed events may coalesce into one pending condition.',
 'If main starves, inspect whether the source remains asserted or an ISR does excessive work. If callbacks are absent, confirm vector names and dispatch before raising interrupt urgency.',
 [['Do pending bits count every event?','Generally no; they represent pending state. Multiple events can coalesce, and the peripheral may have its own counter/FIFO or overrun indication. Design event counting at the appropriate layer.'],['What is priority inversion versus interrupt preemption?','Priority inversion is a higher-priority activity waiting on a resource held by a lower-priority one, often prolonged by intermediate work. Interrupt preemption is CPU execution ordering; changing NVIC priority alone does not solve resource ownership.']]);
detail(18,
 ['An invariant can span several otherwise atomic accesses. The snapshot-and-clear example protects the relationship between observing pending events and removing them from shared state. Preserve the old interrupt mask because the function can be called inside a caller-owned critical region.',
 'Define which agents compete. A local mask protects against selected interrupts on that core; it does not serialize another core, DMA or NMI. Keep protected work short and perform parsing/printing after releasing the mask. Define overflow and overload behavior explicitly.'],
 'Without protection: main reads 3, ISR increments to 4, main clears to 0, and only 3 are processed. With snapshot-and-clear inside the relevant exclusion, the interrupt falls either before the snapshot or after release, and the fourth event belongs to one batch exactly once.',
 ['Use a low-rate F401 timer to increment pending_events and a main-loop consumer that deliberately delays between the unprotected read and clear.','Track produced/processed/pending in a bounded test window and reproduce the lost-update sequence.','Use take_events, snapshot the final accounting while the producer is stopped, and verify equality.'],
 ['Repeat the single-core experiment on H743 M7 without another core writing the counters.','Measure the interrupt-off region using a GPIO pulse; keep only snapshot/clear inside it.','On a dual-core board, document a separate hardware-semaphore/ownership design before sharing the same counter; do not present PRIMASK as inter-core protection.'],
 'The protected version preserves event accounting within the no-overflow test bound. The critical-section duration is measured and does not include slow processing.',
 'If the equality still fails, synchronize the final measurement, account for in-flight events and inspect overflow. Multiple independent debugger reads are not an atomic snapshot of three changing counters.',
 [['Why can aligned loads being atomic still leave a race?','Atomic individual loads/stores do not make a read-modify-write sequence or a multi-variable invariant atomic. The competing access can occur between operations.'],['How would you reduce a long critical section?','Transfer or snapshot a bounded piece of state under protection, then process it outside. For a buffer, use explicit ownership or a suitable queue so the producer cannot modify the consumer-owned data.']]);
detail(19,
 ['Distinguish service execution time from response time. A 20-microsecond operation can miss a 100-microsecond end-to-end deadline after 90 microseconds waiting for CPU service. Instrument both arrival and completion when validating system deadlines.',
 'For cycle measurements, confirm the actual core clock and whether the counter runs in the relevant state. Use unsigned subtraction only when the elapsed interval is shorter than one full counter period. For GPIO timing, define whether measured edges include interrupt entry and pin-write overhead.'],
 '8,400 cycles at 84 MHz equals 100 microseconds. A 32-bit counter wraps after about 51.131 seconds at 84 MHz and 10.737 seconds at 400 MHz. These are arithmetic examples; an H7 board must actually be configured and rated for the chosen frequency.',
 ['Measure an observable bounded F401 workload with CYCCNT and record empty-region overhead.','Pulse a spare schematic-verified GPIO around the same work and compare analyzer time with cycle conversion.','Run at intended interrupt load, recording maximum observed elapsed time, sample count and missed deadlines.'],
 ['Verify H743 clock, voltage/flash setup from generated configuration before using it in timing conversion.','Measure warm-cache and documented cold-start conditions separately; retain the code/data placement.','Introduce bounded DMA traffic to study contention without changing the workload, and record sample count and observed maxima.'],
 'Independent measurements agree within quantified overhead. Results report conditions and maxima observed, without claiming a formal worst-case bound.',
 'If CYCCNT stays zero, verify trace/counter enable and device lock behavior. If measured work disappears in -O2, retain an observable output rather than disabling optimization for a production timing claim.',
 [['Why report percentiles and maximum together?','Percentiles describe distribution, while a deadline can be violated by a rare tail event. Neither an average nor a percentile alone establishes a hard bound; include sample count, maximum and missed-deadline count.'],['Can a cycle count be converted to wall time across a clock switch?','Not with one fixed frequency. Split the interval by clock state or use an independent timebase whose behavior across clock and sleep changes is documented.']]);
detail(20,
 ['Separate byte transport, packet framing and ownership. Circular DMA produces a write position, not complete packets. IDLE indicates a pause, which can occur inside a packet or between several packets. Parse an explicit length/delimiter/checksum protocol and preserve incomplete input across events.',
 'For a circular buffer of N bytes, a modulo position delta cannot reveal an unobserved full lap. Poll/service fast enough with a justified bound, or maintain reliable wrap/half/full accounting and an overload policy. On M7, cache maintenance must occur only on regions whose CPU/DMA ownership has actually been transferred.'],
 'At 115200 baud and 8N1, 11,520 bytes/s fills 256 bytes in about 22.22 ms. If usable space is 255 bytes, the budget is about 22.14 ms before considering jitter and processing. A 30 ms consumer blackout can lose data even if every DMA completion interrupt works.',
 ['First use F401 interrupt reception into an owned bounded queue; verify sequence IDs, errors and overflow count.','Extend to normal-mode DMA for a fixed frame length and hand the completed buffer to the parser before rearming it.','Only then implement circular buffering with explicit partial-frame handling and service-latency bounds.'],
 ['Use H743 normal-mode RX DMA into a dedicated 32-byte-aligned, whole-line-sized buffer in verified DMA-reachable SRAM.','For RX, prepare cache lines before DMA ownership and invalidate after confirmed completion before CPU reads; prevent CPU writes during DMA ownership. For TX, clean completed CPU writes before starting DMA.','Extend to ping-pong buffers only with explicit completion/ownership per buffer. Treat circular cache maintenance as a further design requiring safe completed subranges.'],
 'Sequence/checksum tests distinguish transport loss from malformed frames. H7 completed RX data is fresh, and no buffer is rearmed while the parser still owns it.',
 'If DMA does not move, check request mapping, stream/channel configuration, UART DMA enable and memory reachability. If DMA moves but CPU sees old data, investigate cache handoff. If both are correct, inspect parser framing and delayed-consumer overrun.',
 [['Why can two IDLE events fail to define two packets?','IDLE describes line timing, not the protocol contract. A sender may pause mid-frame or concatenate frames before pausing. Use explicit framing and validate length/checksum independently.'],['What differs between DMA RX and TX cache handoff?','Before TX, dirty CPU cache data must reach RAM before DMA reads it. For RX, prevent dirty-line conflicts before DMA and invalidate completed DMA-written lines before CPU reads. Both require alignment, memory reachability and exclusive ownership.']]);
detail(21,
 ['Write each test as setup, stimulus, oracle and cleanup. Test the state after rejection, not just the return value. A malformed packet that returns an error after partially changing configuration is still a defect.',
 'Partition input space into meaningful classes: valid minimum/maximum, too short/long, inconsistent metadata, checksum error and transport interruption. Add combinations selectively where interactions matter. A host pure-function test covers logic, while target integration covers clocks, interrupts and memory behavior.'],
 'For payload lengths 1..64, the four boundary representatives 0,1,64,65 cover just below minimum, minimum, maximum and just above maximum. Add available length smaller than claimed length to cover a different dimension, even if claimed length is otherwise valid.',
 ['Run the supplied validator harness with assertions enabled on a host, then call equivalent test cases in a F401 scratch image.','Verify parser output remains unchanged after invalid input and exactly matches a known structure after valid input.','Deliver the same frames through UART and compare transport counts with parser outcomes.'],
 ['Repeat pure-function checks on H743 with the same fixtures.','Run UART integration first without DMA, then with a validated DMA/cache handoff. Keep the oracle unchanged across transports.','Record which cases prove logic correctness and which add hardware integration evidence.'],
 'Each input class has an explicit result and post-state oracle. Host success and board integration results are reported separately.',
 'If assertions never fail even after deliberately changing an expected value in the test harness, inspect NDEBUG and confirm the harness is actually called. Remove the test mutation after validating the test can detect failure.',
 [['What is a test oracle?','A rule that distinguishes correct from incorrect outcomes, including output, state, timing or errors. It must be defined before the run so a convenient observed result is not accepted after the fact.'],['How do you test the test?','Introduce a controlled known defect or an intentionally incorrect expected result in an isolated test revision and confirm failure. Restore it and retain meaningful pre-fix regression evidence.']]);
detail(22,
 ['Inject a fault at a named interface with an explicit schedule and recovery oracle. Timeout, stale value, corrupt payload and missing completion test different assumptions. Keep injection counters separate from product error counters so the expected/actual detection relationship can be checked.',
 'A production recovery policy needs bounded retries, a state transition and a defined output when data is unavailable. Repeated retries inside an ISR or high-priority loop can make one failed device starve the whole system.'],
 'For 100 valid-pointer calls to the every-tenth-call stub, ten failures should be injected and 90 successful samples returned. If the application retries, count physical wrapper calls separately from top-level requests; retries otherwise change the expected denominator.',
 ['Place the sensor stub behind a F401 application interface; count attempts, injected failures, detected failures and recoveries.','Run 100 calls without retries and verify the 90/10 split; then add a bounded retry policy as a separate experiment.','Assert that a stale output is marked unavailable and that the next successful sample clears the error state.'],
 ['Repeat the same stub schedule on H743 without depending on a real sensor.','Add delayed completion with a timer-based test harness, avoiding busy waiting in the ISR.','After recovery is validated, reconnect the real interface and confirm the production build excludes injection.'],
 'Detections match injections under the specified policy, stale data is not labeled fresh, and normal operation resumes after the next valid sample.',
 'If failure counts differ, inspect wrapper retry counts and null-pointer validation before assuming missed detection. If injection remains enabled after the test, fix build configuration and rerun the production artifact.',
 [['How do you inject a lost completion safely?','In an isolated harness, suppress the completion notification while keeping a bounded application timeout and a reset/recovery path. Do not corrupt arbitrary memory or silently leave a peripheral-owned buffer in use.'],['Why test repeated failures as well as one failure?','A single retry path may work while repeated failures leak resources, overflow counters, exhaust queues or starve other work. Validate bounded behavior and eventual recovery.']]);
detail(23,
 ['A regression case should target the invariant that failed and retain the smallest useful reproducer. Add surrounding legal and illegal cases because a narrow patch can make the reproducer pass by disabling useful behavior. Keep deterministic fixtures versioned with the source.',
 'Define the supported configuration matrix: board, production flags, transport mode, reset type and load. Not every permutation needs a separate manual run, but every claimed configuration needs evidence or an explicit untested label. Do not compare two builds with different stimuli and call it before/after validation.'],
 'For a corrected 64-byte buffer path, replay lengths 63,64,65 and a burst of repeated invalid frames followed by one valid frame. Passing means legal frames survive, overflow is counted, invalid state does not leak and recovery works; zero accepted frames is a failure.',
 ['Run the defect reproducer against an isolated pre-fix revision or host equivalent, preserving its failure output.','Flash the corrected F401 production build and replay the identical seed, lengths and rate.','Run boundary, recovery and long-run throughput tests; archive ELF/map and the result table.'],
 ['Run the same logical fixtures on H743 with both the baseline transport and intended DMA/cache configuration.','Include buffer-memory placement and cache policy in the test configuration identity.','Repeat cold and warm start cases where startup/retained state is relevant; label unsupported board variants as untested.'],
 'The original case fails before and passes after, valid traffic still succeeds, and evidence is associated with each tested production artifact.',
 'If the original failure cannot be reproduced on the pre-fix build, do not claim the new test proves the correction. Check stimulus fidelity and environment, and document any different mechanism that the test actually covers.',
 [['How do you avoid flaky regression tests?','Control stimuli and initialization, synchronize observations, define timing tolerances from requirements and capture enough evidence on failure. Do not hide intermittent failures with automatic retries that discard the first result.'],['What belongs in a release sign-off?','The exact artifact identity, supported configurations, executed results, unresolved failures and limits. Passing host tests alone does not validate a changed DMA or clock configuration.']]);
detail(24,
 ['Lead with the defect and verified outcome so a reviewer can decide whether the evidence answers the problem. Follow with reproducible details and a short causal chain. Distinguish measurements, interpretations and assumptions; include unresolved alternatives that materially limit the conclusion.',
 'Use portable artifacts: firmware commit, ELF/map identity, input fixtures, logs and diagrams with units and timestamps. A screenshot without the triggering conditions and matching binary cannot support independent reproduction. Include a clear acceptance criterion and whether it was actually tested.'],
 'Example report result: “With 65-byte requests into a 64-byte destination, the old path violated its bounds contract. The correction rejects the extra byte before mutation; 63/64-byte requests still pass. Host tests passed; F401 and H743 target runs are pending.” This is stronger than inventing hardware pass results.',
 ['Choose one F401 lab you actually ran and record board revision, source commit, compiler flags, stimulus and reset method.','Write a table of expected/actual results and link the matching artifacts.','Ask whether a reviewer could distinguish your measured evidence from the tutorial arithmetic example without speaking to you.'],
 ['Write a separate H743 result row with core, clock, RAM placement and cache/MPU assumptions where relevant.','If target access is unavailable, mark that row not run and retain the exact procedure for the future test.','Explain whether the same correction is logically shared across boards or whether hardware-specific evidence is still required.'],
 'The report identifies what was verified, what was inferred and what remains untested. Another engineer can follow its procedure without guessing build or board configuration.',
 'If the report becomes a chronological diary, rewrite around the causal chain and final evidence. Keep rejected experiments only where they justify the conclusion or an important design tradeoff.',
 [['How do you communicate an unverified hypothesis to a reviewer?','Label it explicitly, name the observation that motivated it and specify an experiment that could reject it. Do not place it in the root-cause conclusion until evidence supports the mechanism.'],['What should an interview case study quantify?','Use relevant measurements such as failure rate, stimulus rate, loss count, observed latency and regression exposure. Include conditions and limits so numbers convey evidence rather than unsupported confidence.']]);

for (const d of lessonDetails) {
 const l=lessons.find(item=>item.id===d.id);
 if(!l) throw new Error(`Unknown lesson ${d.id}`);
 l.detail=d;
 l.h7=d.boards.h7.join(' ');
}
references.push(['ST PM0253 - Cortex-M7 programming manual','https://www.st.com/resource/en/programming_manual/pm0253-stm32f7-series-and-stm32h7-series-cortexm7-processor-programming-manual-stmicroelectronics.pdf']);
references.push(['ST RM0368 - STM32F401 reference manual','https://www.st.com/resource/en/reference_manual/rm0368-stm32f401xb-c-d-e-mb-reference-manual-stmicroelectronics.pdf']);
references.push(['FreeRTOS - Queue ownership and API rules','https://www.freertos.org/Documentation/02-Kernel/02-Kernel-features/02-Queues-mutexes-and-semaphores/01-Queues']);

// Complete the formerly unresolved capture_fault example with a bounded decoder.
// Caller supplies the raw exception-entry SP, captured EXC_RETURN/CFSR and actual RAM bounds.
lessons.find(l=>l.id===11).code = `#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
typedef struct { uint32_t pc, lr, xpsr; } core_context_t;
bool decode_core_frame(uintptr_t raw_sp, uint32_t exc_return,
                       uint32_t cfsr, uintptr_t ram_begin,
                       uintptr_t ram_end, core_context_t *out) {
    /* Conservative rejection: memory/bus stacking, unstacking or FP errors. */
    if (!out || (cfsr & 0x00003838U) != 0U) return false;
    if ((raw_sp & 3U) != 0U || ram_begin >= ram_end) return false;
    uintptr_t core_offset = (exc_return & (1U << 4)) ? 0U : 72U;
    uintptr_t required = core_offset + 32U;
    if (raw_sp < ram_begin || raw_sp > ram_end) return false;
    if (required > ram_end - raw_sp) return false;
    const volatile uint32_t *frame =
        (const volatile uint32_t *)(raw_sp + core_offset);
    uint32_t xpsr = frame[7];
    if ((xpsr & (1U << 24)) == 0U) return false; /* Thumb state */
    out->lr = frame[5]; out->pc = frame[6]; out->xpsr = xpsr;
    return true;
}
/* This helper does not install a fault handler or choose MSP/PSP.
   Supply validated, readable RAM bounds for this exact target.
   Capture entry SP/EXC_RETURN before a C prologue alters the stack.
   Decode in a controlled lab; do not printf/allocate in a crash handler.
   It does not reconstruct the pre-exception SP or validate a whole backtrace. */`;
